  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through next Thursday)\nIssued at 1125 AM CST Fri Nov 13 2026\n\nA Continental Polar air mass continues to bring cold and dry air\nto the Mid-South today. Early morning surface observations depict\ndewpoints in the upper 20s to lower 30s, which is about\n15 degrees different from this time yesterday. This air mass will\npersist into the weekend as broad troughing continues to\nencompass eastern CONUS. Tonight, an areawide freeze is forecast\nwith lows spanning the upper 20s to lower 30s. The chilliest\nconditions will manifest over west Tennessee, where a hard freeze\n(temperatures below 28 degrees) is forecast. Chilly mornings with\nafternoon highs in the 50s will be in place through Sunday.\n\nBy early next week, the synoptic-level pattern will begin to\nshift as an upper level low develops over the Four Corners\nRegion. This feature will dive southeast and clip the upper\nMississippi Delta late Sunday into Monday. Given the presence of\ndry air over the Mid-South, widespread showers are not\nanticipated with this system. A few drops may be able to squeeze\nthrough to the surface in the Delta early Monday, but totals will\nbe negligible. Gradual air mass modification will occur through\nthe middle of next week as upper level ridging takes shape.\nTemperatures will warm into the 60s by Wednesday with 70s\nforecast by next Friday. Dry conditions will remain, likely\nworsening the current drought.\n\nLong-range guidance is starting to hint at a system developing\nover the Plains next weekend. If this manifests, it would provide\nour next shot at rain. However, there are significant\ninconsistencies regarding this system, so for now expect dry\nconditions for the foreseeable future.",
